Security audit · 2026-11-21 10:00 UTC
This audit reads the configuration and state of the devices sentry manages. It doesn't attack anything and doesn't look inside your traffic.
It can't see inside devices sentry doesn't manage (phones, TVs, cameras): only that they are there.
It can't stop someone being tricked into giving a password away or installing something. It makes that cost less: fewer doors, fewer old holes, less to reach from one device.

Looked at: myserver, home
sentry only looks while it is open: nothing of it runs in the background. Without it: CrowdSec on myserver bans attackers and the router blocks them; myserver installs security updates by itself.

Do these first (5)
  (the 5 most pressing; 2 more as pressing come first under Worth doing)
  (what the internet can reach is read from the router's settings, not seen from outside)
  1. web 1.4 on myserver is open to the internet (tcp port 443) and is missing security fixes that are already out
      Why: Published holes in software the internet can reach are found and tried by automated attacks within days, sometimes hours.
      What it takes: Update it now (its row on the server's Maintenance screen), or close the door on the router until it is.
      · the router forwards tcp 443 to myserver port 8443
      · answered by the program docker-proxy
      · the container web runs the image example/web:1.4
      · web: running 1.4
      · its version is still supported
      · asked once from this machine, without a login: it shows a page without asking for a login
  2. The router's firewall accepts whatever the internet sends to the router itself
      Why: Its admin page, ssh and every other service on it are then on the internet.
      What it takes: Set the router back to refusing what the internet sends it (the internet side's input: REJECT, its default).
  3. The certificate the internet sees on port 443 has expired
      Why: Browsers and apps warn or refuse to connect. People learn to click the warning away, which is what someone posing as your server needs.
      What it takes: Renew it, and have it renew by itself; a session with Claude can find what issues it.
      · the certificate is self-signed
      · it expired 3 days ago
  4. Wi-Fi 1 has no password: anyone nearby can join
      Why: Whoever joins is inside your network, past the router's firewall.
      What it takes: Set it to WPA2 or WPA3 with a long passphrase.
      · Wi-Fi 1: encryption none
  5. The router's firmware is no longer supported: it gets no more security fixes
      Why: Holes found from now on stay open, on the device that faces the internet.
      What it takes: Upgrade to a supported release (back the configuration up first).
      · installed: 25.12.3
      · latest of its series: 25.12.3
      · support ended 3 days ago

Worth doing (2)

  As pressing as those first, after the five
  - What the internet can reach (the dmz network) is allowed to open connections into lan
      Why: If the server there is broken into, the attacker is one step from every device on those networks.
      What it takes: Remove that forwarding on the router; allow only the single ports it really needs.
      · firewall forwarding dmz → lan
  - myserver's data has no backup
      Why: Ransomware, a mistake or a dead disk then loses it for good.
      What it takes: Set up a backup that leaves the machine (another disk or place), encrypted, on a schedule.
      · you answered no on 2026-11-21

Fine (16)

  Getting in from the internet
  (read from the router's settings, not seen from outside)
  - Over IPv6, the firewall lets nothing from the internet reach devices at home

  Known holes
  - myserver installs security updates by itself
  - myserver's system (Debian 13) gets security fixes until 2030-06-30
  - myserver's system is up to date
  - The router's packages are up to date

  Logins
  - The router's admin page is HTTPS only
  - The router's ssh login takes keys only
  - myserver's ssh login takes keys only
  - On myserver, becoming root takes a password

  Moving around once inside
  - No connection was crossing between parts of the network kept apart (at the moment of the audit)
  - None of the 1 container on myserver has more control of the server than a container needs
  - The network is split into 3 parts (lan, dmz, iot)
  - All 10 devices on the network are ones you named

  Noticing and recovering
  - The router blocks known bad names (DNS blocklist on and fresh)
  - The router's configuration is backed up, encrypted, and matches the newest backup
  - Attackers that CrowdSec catches on myserver are blocked at the router, for every door

Couldn't check (1)

  Noticing and recovering
  - How long myserver keeps its system log couldn't be read
      Why: After a break-in, the log is how you find out what happened.
      What it takes: In server_config.yaml, add `journalctl` to the server's `sudo:` list (sentry's own read-only reads), press I on the server target, then run the audit again (S).

Needs your answer (1)

  Noticing and recovering
  - Have you ever restored from the backup of the router's configuration to check it works?
      Why: sentry can see that a backup exists, not that it can be used.
      What it takes: Answer yes or no.
      Answer: sentry audit security answers restore-tested:net:home yes|no (if you don't know, leave it: it is asked again)
